\subsection*{Fermat Numbers}

Verify that $F_0 = 3$, $F_1 = 5$, and $F_2 = 17$ are prime.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{For $F_0 = 3$:}

3 is prime (divisible only by 1 and 3). \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\textbf{For $F_1 = 5$:}

5 is prime (divisible only by 1 and 5). \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\textbf{For $F_2 = 17$:}

$\sqrt{17} \approx 4.12$

Test primes $\leq 4.12$: 2, 3

\begin{itemize}
    \item $17 \div 2 = 8.5$ \textcolor{red}{$\times$}
    \item $17 \div 3 = 5.67$ \textcolor{red}{$\times$}
\end{itemize}

Therefore, $F_2 = 17$ is prime. \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

Verify the formula for $F_3$ and $F_4$.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{For $F_3$:}
\[
F_0 \cdot F_1 \cdot F_2 + 2 = 3 \times 5 \times 17 + 2 = 255 + 2 = 257 = F_3
\]
\textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\textbf{For $F_4$:}
\[
F_0 \cdot F_1 \cdot F_2 \cdot F_3 + 2 = 3 \times 5 \times 17 \times 257 + 2
\]
\[
= 255 \times 257 + 2 = 65535 + 2 = 65537 = F_4
\]
\textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

Verify that $F_1 | (F_3 - 2)$ and $F_2 | (F_4 - 2)$.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{For $F_1 | (F_3 - 2)$:}

$F_1 = 5$ and $F_3 - 2 = 257 - 2 = 255$

$255 \div 5 = 51$ \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

Indeed, $5 | 255$.

\textbf{For $F_2 | (F_4 - 2)$:}

$F_2 = 17$ and $F_4 - 2 = 65537 - 2 = 65535$

$65535 \div 17 = 3855$ \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

Indeed, $17 | 65535$.

Verify that $\gcd(F_0, F_1) = 1$ and $\gcd(F_1, F_2) = 1$.

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solution:}}

\textbf{For $\gcd(F_0, F_1) = \gcd(3, 5)$:}

Divisors of 3: 1, 3

Divisors of 5: 1, 5

Common divisors: 1

Therefore, $\gcd(3, 5) = 1$ \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\textbf{For $\gcd(F_1, F_2) = \gcd(5, 17)$:}

Since both are prime and distinct:

$\gcd(5, 17) = 1$ \textcolor{green}{\checkmark}

\textcolor{primaryblue}{\faCheckCircle\ \textbf{Summary: Fermat Numbers}}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|p{5cm}|p{7cm}|}
\hline
\rowcolor{primaryblue!20}
\textbf{Property} & \textbf{Statement} \\
\hline
Definition & $F_n = 2^{2^n} + 1$ \\
\hline
Known Fermat Primes & $F_0 = 3, F_1 = 5, F_2 = 17, F_3 = 257, F_4 = 65537$ \\
\hline
Recursive Formula & $F_{m+1} = F_0 F_1 \cdots F_m + 2$ \\
\hline
